Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.890 --> 00:00:09.190 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  49 years ago this month the allman  brothers band released their live album  at fillmore east it was recorded here in  new york and propelled the almonds to  stardom  putting southern rock on the map the  allman brothers were the first act on  capricorn records the label had a  historic run and became known  as the birthplace of southern rock but  later was mostly forgotten  that is until this year when capricorn  reopened for business  [Music]  all of us that worked here and had so  many wonderful times  we feared that it'd just be demolished  inside mercer music at capricorn in  macon georgia  a newly renovated recording studio has  been resurrected  from the dustbin of music history this  is the room  the room wow you know it just floods  back memories  it's where a 20 year old chuck lavelle  would come to work in 1972  as a new member of the allman brothers  band joining the likes of greg allman  butch trucks and dickie bets we would  set up the whole band  we could all see each other and that was  important why well you know to get the  feeling the emotion and you know  watching greg sing  and watching dicky play that guitar am i  allowed to ask you to  play a little show something yeah all  right here we go  some of southern rock's most enduring  hits were made in this room  the product of all night jam sessions  where lavell says they would  literally pick a key and go  so amazing so a little taste of it there  yeah  lavell can still recall the first time  betts strummed the beginnings of the  song jessica  principal melody is  but then you had  and then you had the other parts or you  [Music]  get  the seven and a half minute instrumental  was recorded for the 1973 album brothers  and sisters  [Music]  along with the almonds anthem rambling  man  [Music]  the story of capricorn records is one of  revivals  the lesser known in a constellation of  recording studios in the south  like sun studios in memphis and fame in  muscle shoals alabama  capricorn was based in macon a town 80  miles southeast of atlanta  that happens to be the hometown of  little richard  and otis redding otis was managed by  phil walden who was the owner of  capricorn records and who  built this for otis for him to record  reading would die tragically in a 1967  plane crash  walden was looking for a new act to  manage when he heard dwayne allman's  session work with the likes of aretha  franklin  and wilson pickett and he sought out  dwayne and he said i want to manage you  i don't care what kind of band you put  together it doesn't matter whatever you  want to do  and so dwayne put together the allman  brothers band  dwayne allman would gather an eclectic  ensemble that included  redding's drummer jmo their combination  of  country gospel r b and motown would  blend into the emerging  new sound of southern rock when  capricorn started and the allman  brothers did their first record it  really didn't sell that well  second record really didn't sell that  well so all of us that were involved  here making music  you know we were determined let's keep  that kettle boiling you know let's just  keep at it keep at it keep attic and  then  it was the live at fillmore east record  that really broke for the almond  brothers  just three months later dwayne allman  died  in a motorcycle accident  but the band would regroup making  capricorn the epicenter of southern rock  everyone from joe cocker to leonard  skinnard would record here  [Music]  now i feel like i'm going into an old  record store  basically that story is told in the new  museum at capricorn  a project that took archivist jared  wright nearly a decade to curate  it begins with its roots in seoul people  like otis redding sam and dave  to groups like the marshall tucker band  known for hits like  can't you see  there's even some presidential politics  jimmy carter would visit the studio  before launching his 1976 campaign  piracy and duping of tapes was a really  big problem  when carter was governor phil walden  worked with him to pass that anti-piracy  bill  that protected artists rights carter  credits the label's key financial  support through benefit concerts with  keeping his candidacy alive  and not just the almonds the marshall  tucker band but the whole capricorn  label was kind of behind carter and  helping him run for president  but all this rich history nearly  disappeared  capricorn struggled through the 1980s  the building abandoned in disrepair  was owned by nearby mercer university  there were holes in the floor  see through to the basement the floor  behind us had completely collapsed  the ceiling was starting to come in  and i thought my what a dump  but and then i walked into the studio  yeah  and sort of miraculously it was  the one space that had been perfectly  preserved  mercer president william underwood was  left with a dilemma  i knew that i couldn't tear this  building down  because of you know the great history  here and i also couldn't afford to fix  it  which is a big problem the building  would change hands numerous times  until a developer finally gifted it back  to the university  a combination of fundraising and tax  breaks financed a more than  four million dollar renovation the new  capricorn  open to the public in january its two  studios  available to commercial artists  recording albums along with an incubator  space for young musicians  to practice and collaborate why was it  so important to keep  the capricorn sound studios and build  from there  i viewed the history as a way to inspire  the future  and maybe the next otis reading will  come out of that incubator the studio is  sort of  a living testament to the fact that it  can happen here and it has happened here  when you saw that capricorn records was  going to be reborn in a sense  what did you think hallelujah  lavelle is still nostalgic about the  past  i wish i had taken more piano lessons  but he revels  in the here and now it was a magical  magical time during those years  when you're back here do you feel some  of that magic again i do  i do feel the magic it's here it's never  gone away  it just went to sleep for a while  i shouldn't point out we obviously shot  that pre covered pretty cool with the  closest  so chuck lavelle was just about to leave  with the rolling stones because he's  been touring with them since the early  80s he's their sort of de facto musical  director  but he's right there's something you  could feel there i can't describe it but  there was something there with that  history that was so rich you can see it  just on the screen yeah then i wanted to  learn piano all over too right like i  feel like i'm getting elected  going into the music awesome it was  really really really  great um and fun to sort of reminisce  about some of those songs anybody who  loves albums that space of listening to  albums was truly  amazing to have post covert a good place  to visit for  forever yeah postcode

